WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.325
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:06.175 --> 00:00:07.197
GABE WEISS: Hi.

00:00:07.197 --> 00:00:09.030
Welcome to "Architecting
with Google Cloud."

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.650
I'm Gabe Weiss.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.140
Shared file storage
is the unsung hero

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.520
of application development.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.275
Pick the right solution, chances
are, no one's going to notice.

00:00:17.275 --> 00:00:18.900
But pick the wrong
solution, and you'll

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.640
be chasing bottlenecks for days.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.160
No one knows this better than
our partners over at NetApp.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.340
And today, I've got Oliver
Krause, technical marketing

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.220
engineer from NetApp, to tell
us about a great solution

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.259
for Windows file sharing.

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:32.759
Oliver, why don't
you take a second,

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.320
introduce yourself, and
tell us a little bit

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.790
about what you and NetApp do?

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:36.840
OLIVER KRAUSE: Hi, Gabe.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.430
Thanks for having me.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:43.200
So I'm Oliver, located
in Germany near Munich.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.140
Over 20 years ago, I
started at a storage startup

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.730
called NetApp.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.110
It became a unicorn.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.150
And today, it's one
of the biggest storage

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.460
and data management
companies in the world.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.750
I held many different
positions in that company,

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.350
usually in presales
positions, helping customers

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.210
solve their storage problems.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:08.430
And a few years ago, I switched
into our cloud business

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.660
simply to help do new
stuff in the cloud world.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:13.900
GABE WEISS: Glad to have you.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.373
So Oliver, I've got
a Windows application

00:01:16.373 --> 00:01:17.790
and I want to run
it in the Cloud.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.370
But I know there are a ton
of file storage solutions

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:20.940
out there.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.710
Maybe you could tell
us just a little bit

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.680
about what's out there,
and some pros and cons.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.430
OLIVER KRAUSE: Yeah,
in the storage world,

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:31.470
we have three different
kinds of storages.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.900
The first one is a
traditional disk,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.920
like a hard disk or an
SSD, or a USB stick.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.590
It's basically a disk which
you plug into a computer

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.920
into your computer, put a
file system on top of it,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.150
and use it locally.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.960
It's also called block storage.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.150
After some time,
people thought, oh, it

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.540
would be cool if we
could use that file

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:59.070
system on that disk on multiple
computers, so share the data.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.260
And don't share
it on a disk base,

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.370
but share it on a per file base.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:06.210
So basically, file
sharing was invented.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.060
Some file sharing file
systems were invented.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.650
And people were able to use
data on multiple VMs or multiple

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.240
computers in that time.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.950
And then the new
kids on the block

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:21.690
is object storage, like
Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.230
It uses HTTP to store data.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.770
Relatively new, let's say
10 years or something.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:33.280
And it's a great way to
store unstructured data.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.500
But most of the enterprise
applications in enterprise data

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.430
centers still don't
talk object, but we

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:46.545
talk file NAS to share data.

00:02:46.545 --> 00:02:49.080
GABE WEISS: So people just
say, like, just lift and shift.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:49.827
It's so easy.

00:02:49.827 --> 00:02:52.410
But clearly, if your application
doesn't speak object storage,

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:53.730
that's not going to be so easy.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:54.990
So you've been around.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:57.060
What specific
challenges have you

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.610
seen in terms of shifting
a Windows application up

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:00.152
to the cloud?

00:03:00.152 --> 00:03:01.527
OLIVER KRAUSE:
OK, yeah, so let's

00:03:01.527 --> 00:03:03.780
assume we want to do a lift
and shift with the Windows

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:04.680
application.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.260
And that application
requires data

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.370
sharing between multiple
VMs, which is, by the way,

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.890
a very common scenario.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:18.280
To enable that, you first
need to deploy a GCE instance

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:19.200
with Windows--

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.970
with operating system running.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.880
You need to size
your VM incorrectly.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.680
Eventually, you
have to patch it,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.020
keep up with the latest patches.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.160
You have to monitor it.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.255
Maybe the performance
requirements change,

00:03:33.255 --> 00:03:36.580
so we have to resize it.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.700
You have to think about
backup and recovery,

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.170
and maybe even make it highly
available-- so multiple sounds

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:45.650
and so on.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:48.430
And after you did
all of that, then you

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.080
can start to attach a data disk,
put a file system on top of it,

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:56.835
and share it via the SMB
protocol to other machines.

00:03:56.835 --> 00:03:57.910
GABE WEISS: So hang on.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:03:59.230
You mentioned SMB.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.785
Some folks might not
necessarily know what SMB is.

00:04:01.785 --> 00:04:03.160
Could you just
explain real quick

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.050
what that is, and
maybe compare it

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.230
to what folks might be
more familiar with NFS?

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.550
OLIVER KRAUSE: Yeah, so to
share out the local file system,

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.390
which is on a disk, in
a computer, if you want

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:15.850
to share it out to
different computers,

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:22.060
you need a protocol to access
the file system over a network.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.600
In the old days, most
systems have been Unix.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.940
And NFS was invented, Network
File System, which is still

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:35.740
used in Unix-based or
Linux-based systems today,

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.690
and very popular.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.240
In the Windows world,
Microsoft invented

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.640
SMB, which is an abbreviation
for Server Message Block.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.400
In the beginning, it was
not a very good protocol.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.760
But anyway, in the
2000s Microsoft

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:57.130
renamed it CIFS, "sifs," which
stands for Common Internet File

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.510
System.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:02.680
Everything had to be
named internet something.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:07.330
Later on, Microsoft
renamed it back to SMB.

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:12.340
Today, we have SMB version
2.1, and very recently 3.0,

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.710
which are very good
and capable protocols.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:15.550
GABE WEISS: Got it.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.430
And I know, Cloud Volume
Services, or CVS for short,

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:21.880
supports both SMB and NFS.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.710
So this leads nicely into what
we want to talk about today.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.970
So to start, why don't you
just tell us, what is CVS?

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:31.790
OLIVER KRAUSE: CVS is
net shares as a service.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:36.520
You create a volume and
share it via NFS or SMB,

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:39.310
or both, to your clients.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:41.200
And since it's a
service, it is super

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.845
easy to use and to consume,
and trouble-free to manage.

00:05:44.845 --> 00:05:47.290
GABE WEISS: And how does
CVS help us specifically

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:49.375
with a Windows
migration, for example?

00:05:49.375 --> 00:05:50.750
OLIVER KRAUSE:
Yeah, like I said,

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.920
if you do it with
a Windows way, you

00:05:53.920 --> 00:06:01.060
have to manage a Windows VM
with all the adjacent problems.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:05.530
CVS, in contrast, is a service.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.590
So you can easily
create volumes.

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:12.340
You can scale them up and
scale them down, both capacity

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.810
and performance-wise.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:20.380
You pay per gigabyte per
month, just like you do

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.270
with persistent disks and GCS.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.400
You get a bill from
Google instead of NetApp,

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.700
because it's built for Google.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:32.990
Google takes support calls,
so it's supported by Google.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:37.100
And additionally, we
have snapshots built in.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.080
And these snapshots
are different

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:43.100
from the persistent disk
snapshots you might be used to.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.370
So let me quickly elaborate
on these snapshots

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:49.620
and give you three use cases.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:52.110
These snapshots
are instant to do.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:55.350
Within a second, you created
a snapshot, which is basically

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:57.690
a photo of your file system.

00:06:57.690 --> 00:06:59.650
You can do multiple of them.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:03.690
And with that, you can build
a history of your file system,

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.400
like a versioning system.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:08.310
And for the first use
case, let's assume

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.720
you want to upgrade
an application.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.920
And you are concerned that
this upgrade might fail

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:16.080
and it changes
your data, and you

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:19.980
are left with an
unfinished upgrade which

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.660
changed your data.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:25.300
You simply do a snapshot,
which is super-fast.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.720
It's like creating a
backup in a second.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:30.000
You do the upgrade.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.930
If it works fine, delete
the snapshot afterwards.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.570
If it fails, you
have a snapshot,

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:40.480
and can easily restore your data
to the last known good state.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.920
The second use case,
which is likely

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:47.160
the most relevant one
for a lot of customers,

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:53.180
is you can enhance your
backup and recovery

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:55.460
processes with snapshots.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.350
Simply build a
snapshot schedule,

00:07:57.350 --> 00:08:01.770
which takes a snapshot every
day for the last few weeks.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:04.460
And if you delete that
file, you can simply

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:08.600
go back to a snapshot with
a Windows File Explorer,

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:13.250
so the user itself
can do it in the UI.

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:17.270
They can simply go in there and
identify the last good version

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:20.820
of the file, and restore
it back to a file system.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:25.010
So it helps them avoid
a help desk ticket,

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:28.820
and nobody likes help
desk tickets, right?

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:32.020
And the restore is
much, much faster.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:35.220
And you can even go beyond that.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:40.500
Since snapshots are so
lightweight and cheap to do,

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:42.720
simply take them every hour.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:44.280
Take hourly snapshots.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:46.620
And if you now
delete a file, you're

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.140
not only getting a
version from yesterday

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:50.670
from the nightly backup.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:53.440
You can get a version
from last hour,

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:55.540
which is way more current.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:58.080
So that's a super-cool
functionality

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.740
to reduce a recovery
point objective.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:06.150
And the last use case, since we
are talking Windows operating

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:09.175
systems here, the
issue with Windows

00:09:09.175 --> 00:09:14.280
is where it's in
danger that ransomware

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:19.750
infects them and encrypts
all the files on your disk.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:24.110
Since you have snapshots, you
can take them every hour or so.

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:28.120
And you have a very
recent known good state

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.880
where your files have
not been infected.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:34.870
And the cool thing is, you
can really quickly restore

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:40.750
the files individually, or you
can roll back the whole volume.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:45.130
Within a few seconds,
half a minute or so,

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:49.060
you can roll it back to the
state of a last known snapshot,

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:52.810
and basically delete all the
encrypted files by that--

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:53.900
super cool.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:56.560
And last thing,
it doesn't matter

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.150
how big your file system is.

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:01.840
Be it a terabyte or 100
terabyte, the time it takes

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.530
to create a snapshot or
roll back a snapshot,

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:06.550
it's all the same.

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:08.740
GABE WEISS: These snapshots
sound really cool.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.270
Can you make copies of them?

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:13.270
Can you clone them for
other applications?

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:14.590
OLIVER KRAUSE: Yeah, you can.

00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:17.510
Simply create a
snapshot of a volume,

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:20.740
and then create a new
volume from that snapshot.

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:24.670
And by the way, this process
also takes a few seconds.

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:27.503
Let's say you are done
in half a minute or less.

00:10:27.503 --> 00:10:29.170
GABE WEISS: Yeah, so
the developer in me

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:32.290
is imagining, as we were
talking, it popped into my head

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:33.190
of--

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:35.320
we oftentimes, as
application developers,

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:37.360
run into a situation
where you've

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:39.820
got a very specific
configuration of files

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:42.850
on disk that reproduce a
problem for your applications.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.220
And sometimes, reproducing
that is kind of hard.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:47.230
But if you're making
these snapshots,

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:52.417
I can imagine a place where you
get a file system set up where

00:10:52.417 --> 00:10:53.500
it reproduces the problem.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.230
You snapshot it so that you
can make changes, and then

00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:58.030
reset to be able to
reproduce the problem.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:58.967
That's super-cool.

00:10:58.967 --> 00:11:01.300
So Oliver, I'd love to dive
in a little bit deeper here.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:02.800
Could you walk
through maybe where

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:04.467
we can find it,
and then a use case

00:11:04.467 --> 00:11:05.800
or to where we'd want to use it?

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:07.690
OLIVER KRAUSE:
Yeah, the good thing

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:11.110
is it's directly built
into the GCP console.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:15.280
Just open the GCP console,
search for Cloud Volumes.

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:16.240
And it's where?

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:17.800
It's also on the left side.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.260
If you look up all
the services, you

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:24.100
will find it down at the
bottom under Partner Services.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:26.050
You will also find
the documentation

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:29.860
in the Cloud Architecture
Center on cloud.google.com.

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:32.590
Regarding use cases,
I want to present two.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.950
The first use case is a
pretty generic architecture,

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:39.750
which is used on thousands
of legacy applications.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:43.350
You have multiple GCV EVMs
running Windows applications,

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:46.090
and they need a file
share to share data.

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:49.050
So what you do is you
simply provision a CVS share

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:51.750
and map it to the
Windows systems.

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:55.980
And we have multiple
examples where this applies.

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:59.430
The first one would be, for
example, VDI, Virtual Desktop

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:04.830
Infrastructure, where you have
SMB shares for the user home

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:08.730
directories, for group
directories where people

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:11.250
share data between
multiple users,

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.890
and for the profile shares.

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:17.960
Another one would be
automotive and manufacturing.

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:21.780
They're running a lot
of computer-aided design

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:24.750
and computer-aided
engineering applications.

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:28.740
Most of them want to talk to
a file system to share data,

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:32.230
so an SMB share is
the way to go here.

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:36.000
And that applies to many
other industry verticals.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.890
And the last one
I want to mention

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.540
is media and entertainment.

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:42.330
More and more, we
see that these people

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:45.840
have geographically dispersed
teams working together

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:47.250
on a project.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:51.570
And they all need to access the
same data and the same media

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:55.390
assets in a performant
and consistent way.

00:12:55.390 --> 00:12:59.610
To aid these requirements,
we have a solution

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.130
called Global File
Cache, which builds

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:05.280
on top of CloudVolume service.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.020
It helps you to share
out a share globally

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:11.670
to all the different sites--

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:15.480
for example LA,
London, Singapore,

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:20.600
while retaining excess
performance over a VAN network,

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.590
on the other side minimizing
traffic to reduce costs

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:28.220
and enabling global file
locking to avoid conflicts

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:31.040
when multiple
users try to modify

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:32.958
the same file at the same time.

